,title,link,text,language,family
0,Nilo-Saharan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nilo-Saharan_languages,"The Nilo-Saharan languages are a proposed linguistic family consisting of around 210 Native African languages spoken by somewhere around 70 million speakers, mainly in the upper parts of the Chari and Nile rivers, including historic Nubia, north of where the two tributaries of the Nile meet.",Efe,Nilo-Saharan
1,Nilo-Saharan | Ethnologue Free,https://www.ethnologue.com/subgroup/39/?cf=1&token=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..qiUd4igQzzpr0OZ7p1U9JXx2uiCs003pCBFXnawm7Uo,"Nilo-Saharan. Subgroup of 210 languages.Efe efe, a language of Democratic Republic of the Congo.",Efe,Nilo-Saharan
2,Nilo-Saharian languages - Sorosoro Sorosoro,https://www.sorosoro.org/en/nilo-saharian-languages/,"Where are Nilo-Saharan languages spoken?“Nilo-Saharan and its limits”. In Jean-Marie Hombert and Gerard Philippson (eds.), The Genetic Classification of African Languages Revisited.",Efe,Nilo-Saharan
3,Efe - efe (Nilo-Saharan) - PeopleGroups.org,https://peoplegroups.org/language/efe/,"Efe (efe) is part of the Nilo-Saharan family and Nilo-Saharan, Satellite-Core, Satellites, Central Sudanic, East, Mangbutu-Efe class.",Efe,Nilo-Saharan
4,Nilo-Saharan Languages - SourceWatch,https://www.sourcewatch.org/index.php/Nilo-Saharan_Languages,"Nilo-Saharan Languages are a family of 205 languages. The most common are Dholuo in Kenya and Kanuri in Nigeria with 3.5 million speakers each.Mangbutu-Efe: Efe, Lese, Mamvu, Mangbutu, and Mvuba (all spoken in the Democratic Republic of the Congo) and Ndo (Uganda).",Efe,Nilo-Saharan
5,"Ethiopian Languages - Semitic, Cushitic, Omotic and Nilo-Saharan",https://ethiopiantreasures.co.uk/pages/language.htm,Nilo-Saharan. The Nilo-Saharan languages are largely spoken in the western part of the country along the border with Sudan (mainly in Gambella and Benshangul regions).,Efe,Nilo-Saharan
6,(PDF) Nilo-Saharan Languages,https://www.researchgate.net/publication/304040925_Nilo-Saharan_Languages,"Nilo-Saharan is essentially Greenberg's earlier Macro-Sudanic (or Chari-Nile) family, together with languages and language groups formerly assigned to historically isolated units.",Efe,Nilo-Saharan
7,(PDF) In defence of Nilo --Saharan Saharan,https://www.academia.edu/104666399/In_defence_of_Nilo_Saharan_Saharan,The concept of Nilo-Saharan in its modern form we owe to Joseph Greenberg (1963). Lionel Bender (1934-2008) was its most consistent defender and his 1997 monograph remains the standard for the mainstream view.,Efe,Nilo-Saharan
8,La famille nilo-saharienne,https://www.axl.cefan.ulaval.ca/monde/famnilo-saharienne.htm,"La famille nilo-saharienne ou nilotique comprend près d'une dizaine de groupes linguistiques dans lesquels sont répartis près de 200 langues parlées par quelque 26 millions de personnes résidant principalement au Niger, au Soudan, en Éthiopie, au Tchad et au Congo-Kinshasa.",Efe,Nilo-Saharan
9,Nilo,http://www.languagesgulper.com/eng/Nilo.html,"Overview and Distribution. Nilo-Saharan is one of the four major African language phyla, together with Afro-Asiatic, Niger-Congo and Khoisan.",Efe,Nilo-Saharan
